window.docContents[142].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Weak-boundary-value-problems--Positive-mass-and-lower-order-terms":{"contents":"The modified Helmholtz equation\n\n\n\nis solved on every open measurable domain, with no boundedness assumption.\nThe mass term directly controls the base  coordinate, so coercivity does\nnot use Poincaré. The variational and distributional formulations are proved\nequivalent, and existence and uniqueness hold for every  forcing.\n\nFor full divergence-form operators, first- and zeroth-order terms are allowed\nas bounded operators, with  multiplication fields as the local\nspecialization. Every continuous graph-space load is represented in the\nstandard form\n\n\n\nand the chosen coordinates satisfy an exact squared-norm identity.\n\nThe full unshifted form need not be coercive. Let  be the\nprincipal ellipticity constant, and let  bound the\nfirst- and zeroth-order operator norms, respectively. For multiplication\nfields, these bounds are supplied by the  coefficient norms.\nGårding's estimate gives the threshold\n\n\n\nEvery  makes the shifted form coercive with lower bound\n. On an open measurable domain, each standard  load then\nhas a unique shifted weak solution and an explicit graph-norm estimate. No\nbounded-domain hypothesis is needed for this shifted theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Weak boundary-value problems","header":"5.7.1.2.2. Positive mass and lower-order terms","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Weak-boundary-value-problems--Positive-mass-and-lower-order-terms"},"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus","header":"3.3.2. Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry"},"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Why-distributions-matter":{"contents":"Solutions of hyperbolic PDEs can develop shocks (discontinuities in finite time), elliptic\nregularity theory must handle forcing terms in  rather than ,\nand fundamental solutions like the Newtonian potential are singular at the\norigin. The theory of distributions, created by Laurent Schwartz (1950,\nThéorie des distributions), extends differentiation to all these objects by\nreplacing the pointwise derivative with a duality pairing against smooth test\nfunctions.\n\nThe starting point is integration by parts. If  is smooth and  is\ncompactly supported, then  with no boundary\nterms. The right-hand side makes sense even when  is merely locally\nintegrable, or even when it is not a function at all but only a linear\nfunctional on test functions. By declaring the right-hand side to be the\ndefinition of the distributional derivative, one obtains a differentiation\ntheory that:\n\n* applies to every locally integrable function, including those with jumps;* produces the Dirac mass as the derivative of the Heaviside step function;* supplies the language in which singular fundamental solutions are stated;* supports Fourier analysis after passing to tempered distributions.\n\nA test function is a smooth, localized probe. The Dirac mass records its\nvalue at one point: . Its derivative records\nthe negative slope there:\n. Neither operation asks for a pointwise\nvalue of the distribution itself. Equality of distributions means agreement\non every such probe.\n\nApproximation is the other route to the same objects. Convolve a rough\nfunction with a smooth kernel of shrinking width and you get smooth\nfunctions converging to it, which is how the Sobolev chapter carries\nclassical calculus into the weak setting.\n\n\n\nAt the jump, each smoothed profile is continuous while the limiting step is\nnot. We therefore need a mode of convergence that tolerates this discrepancy:\nlocal integral convergence, or convergence after pairing with a test function.\nThis is why the weak-derivative argument later estimates the approximation\nin a norm instead of requiring uniform agreement at every point.\n\n treats mollification in Appendix C.5;\nthe theory of distributions is due to Schwartz (1950, 1951). We follow Mathlib's\nformalization, which constructs test functions as smooth compactly supported\nfunctions and distributions as their continuous dual.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.1. Why distributions matter","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Why-distributions-matter"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Scalar-Characteristics":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.2.5. Scalar Characteristics","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Scalar-Characteristics"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Passing-nonlinear-terms-to-the-limit":{"contents":"Compactness becomes useful for a nonlinear PDE only after one can identify\nthe limit of its nonlinear terms. The basic limit passages that follow an\nAubin–Lions argument concern bilinear products, trilinear forms, and duality\npairings.\n\nFor scalar functions, the issue is already visible in squaring. The sequence\n, indexed by positive integers, converges weakly to zero in\n, but its square has integral  for every . A weak\nlimit therefore cannot simply be substituted into a nonlinear term.\nStrong  convergence repairs this:\n\n\n\nHere we used  and Cauchy–Schwarz. The same\nsplitting works for any continuous bilinear map. For a PDE application, the\ncompactness must occur in the norms needed by its particular nonlinearity.\n\nLet  be a continuous bilinear map, and suppose\n satisfyIf  in  and\n in , thenIn particular, taking  and , strong  convergence implies\n strongly in . This passes quadratic terms to the limit once compactness gives strong\n convergence.\n\nSplit the difference asThe Bochner Hölder estimate bounds its  norm byBoth terms tend to zero. Formally, the Hölder operation is itself a\ncontinuous bilinear map between the three Bochner spaces, so the result is\nalso an immediate continuity argument. The quadratic  statement is the\nspecialization , , and .\n\nProduct limits identify quadratic terms once strong convergence is known.\nThe convective terms of fluid equations are trilinear, and their first\nproperty is algebraic rather than topological: skew-symmetry in the last two\narguments makes the nonlinearity invisible to the energy identity and yields\na usable difference formula for two states.\n\nLet  be a continuous trilinear form that\nis skew in its last two arguments:Then , and in particular . Thus a nonlinear\nevolution equation tested against its state has the same energy balance as\nits linear coercive part. The same cancellation gives the difference\nidentityIf , the right-hand side is bounded by\n. This is the abstract algebra behind convective energy\ncancellation and many weak-uniqueness estimates. The same product bound shows\nthat the dual-valued quadratic map  is Lipschitz on\nbounded subsets of .\n\nSkew-symmetry with  gives , hence the value is\nzero over . Substituting this identity removes the cubic term\nfrom the tested evolution equation. For the difference formula, write\n with , expand by trilinearity, and cancel the terms\n and . The product bound applied to\n gives the quantitative estimate.\n\nCancellation controls the energy of the approximations; passing the\ntrilinear term itself to the limit is a separate question. Aubin–Lions gives\ncompactness in , and the limit passage needs strong convergence\nof two factors in norms in which the trilinear form is continuous.\n\nLet  be a continuous trilinear form.\nSupposewith the first two convergences strong and the weakly convergent family\n uniformly bounded. ThenIn particular, strong convergence of  and bounded weak convergence of\n identify the quadratic expression . This is the\nabstract strong–strong–weak passage used after compactness has supplied the\nstrong factors.\n\nThe functionals  converge strongly in  by continuity\nof the first two slots. SplitUniform boundedness of  sends the first term to zero, while weak\nconvergence tested against the fixed functional  sends the\nsecond term to zero.\n\nThe same weak–strong principle is needed in Bochner form for the duality\npairing between weakly convergent dual derivatives  and strongly\nconvergent states, the pairing that appears when an energy identity is\npassed to the limit. The pointwise Riesz map reduces it to a Hilbert-space\ninner product of a weakly convergent factor with a strongly convergent one.\n\nLet  be a real Hilbert space. Suppose  weakly in\n, the Riesz representatives of  are uniformly bounded,\nand  strongly in . ThenThe explicit bound is retained in the formal statement because it is valid\nfor arbitrary filters; ordinary weakly convergent sequences supply that bound\nautomatically.\n\nUse the pointwise Riesz map to rewrite each integral as the real\n inner product of the Riesz representative of  with .\nThe generic weak–strong inner-product theorem then passes to the limit:\nthe fixed-test term follows from weak convergence, and the varying-test error\nis controlled by the uniform norm bound times .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Aubin–Lions Compactness","header":"6.3.1.10.5. Passing nonlinear terms to the limit","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Passing-nonlinear-terms-to-the-limit"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points","header":"9.6. Fixed Points and Convex Regularization","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization"}});